NCSEL(1)                     User Commands                     NCSEL(1)

NAME
     ncsel — ncurses-based terminal selector

SYNOPSIS
     ncsel [-bhmsn] [-f file] [file ...]

DESCRIPTION
     ncsel is an ncurses-based pager and selector. It reads input from
     a pipe or from a file and displays the content in an
     interactive terminal interface. The user can move through the
     list, select entries, and return the selected line(s) or line
     number(s) to the calling script.

     The tool is intended for use in POSIX shell scripts where an
     interactive selector is useful. Depending on the options, ncsel
     can return selected lines, their line numbers, or both. When
     multiselect mode is enabled, multiple entries may be marked and
     returned.

     ncsel requires ncurses or ncursesw.

OPTIONS
     -b      Display the selection using reverse video instead of a
             cursor-style highlight.

     -h      Show usage information.

     -m      Enable multiselect mode. When enabled, entries may be
             marked or unmarked.

     -n      Return only the line number of the selected entry.

     -s      Return only the selected line itself.

             By default, both the line number and the line content are
             returned.

     -f file
             Read input from file. Use “-” to read from standard input.
             If no file is given, standard input is used.

INTERACTIVE KEYS
     While viewing a list, the following keys are available:

           f, ^F, SPACE, PageDown   Move forward one window
           b, ^B, ESC-v, PageUp     Move backward one window
           j, ^N, Down              Move down one line
           k, ^P, Up                Move up one line

           Enter                    Select and exit
           q                        Exit without selection (return 0)
           x                        Exit without selection (return -1)
           m, +                     Toggle mark (multiselect mode only)
           !                        Execute a command. The variable %S 
                                    contains the line the cursor is on. 
                                    Example: printf "%S" | xclip

     Exiting with ‘x’ returns -1 and can be used as a “go back” signal
     in scripts.

EXAMPLES
     Select a file from a directory listing:

           ls -a . | ncsel -

     Select from a list of strings:

           printf "%s\n%s\n%s\n" "One" "Two" "Three" | ncsel -bf -

BUGS
     Probably.

